General Motors operates a nationwide manufacturing network that produces everything from electric vehicles and performance cars to heavy-duty trucks and full-size SUVs. Here’s where every U.S. assembly plant is located and what each one builds.
GM assembly plants
play a critical role in producing the company’s extensive lineup of Chevrolet, Cadillac, Buick and GMC vehicles. Spread across several states, these facilities manufacture everything from electric vehicles and sports cars to pickups, SUVs and commercial vans.
Moreover, each plant specializes in specific models while supporting one of the largest automotive manufacturing networks in North America.
Indiana: Home of GM’s Full-Size Light-Duty Pickups
Fort Wayne Assembly
, located in
Roanoke, Indiana
, has been producing full-size pickup trucks since 1986.
Today, the plant builds the
Chevrolet Silverado 1500
and
GMC Sierra 1500
, including work-oriented trims as well as premium models like the Sierra Denali. As one of GM’s highest-volume truck facilities, Fort Wayne remains essential to the company’s pickup lineup.
Fort Wayne Assembly builds the Chevrolet Silverado 1500 and GMC Sierra 1500.
Kansas: GM’s Electric Vehicle Manufacturing Hub
Located in
Kansas City, Kansas
,
Fairfax Assembly
currently produces the
Chevrolet Bolt
.
The facility has been part of GM’s manufacturing network for decades and has assembled numerous popular models over the years. Furthermore, Fairfax is expected to add production of the gasoline-powered
Chevrolet Equinox
in the near future, expanding its manufacturing portfolio.
Kentucky: The Home of the Chevrolet Corvette
Few
GM assembly plants
are as iconic as
Bowling Green Assembly
in
Kentucky
.
The facility has exclusively produced the
Chevrolet Corvette
since 1981. Additionally, it houses GM’s
Performance Build Center
, where Corvette engines are hand-assembled by specially trained technicians.
Bowling Green Assembly has been the exclusive home of the Chevrolet Corvette for decades.
Michigan: The Largest Concentration of GM Assembly Plants
Michigan remains the heart of General Motors manufacturing, with several major assembly facilities.
Factory ZERO Detroit-Hamtramck
focuses entirely on electric vehicles, producing the
Cadillac Escalade IQ
,
Chevrolet Silverado EV
,
GMC Sierra EV
, and both the
GMC HUMMER EV Pickup
and
SUV
.
Meanwhile,
Flint Assembly
builds the
Chevrolet Silverado HD
and
GMC Sierra HD
, continuing a manufacturing tradition that spans more than a century.
Additionally,
Lansing Delta Township Assembly
produces three of GM’s popular crossover SUVs: the
Buick Enclave
,
Chevrolet Traverse
, and
GMC Acadia
. The facility is also recognized for its environmentally friendly design and sustainability initiatives.
Finally,
Lansing Grand River Assembly
manufactures the
Cadillac CT5
. The plant is scheduled to undergo retooling for future products beginning in late 2026 while continuing GM’s investment in renewable energy.
Missouri: Trucks and Commercial Vans
Wentzville Assembly
, located in
Missouri
, builds several of GM’s hardest-working vehicles.
The plant manufactures the
Chevrolet Colorado
,
GMC Canyon
,
Chevrolet Express
, and
GMC Savana
. Consequently, it plays a key role in supplying both commercial fleets and pickup truck buyers.
Wentzville Assembly produces midsize pickups alongside GM’s full-size commercial vans.
Tennessee: Cadillac Gas and EV Production
Spring Hill Manufacturing
in
Tennessee
assembles multiple Cadillac models on the same production line.
The facility currently builds the
Cadillac XT5
,
Cadillac LYRIQ
, and
Cadillac VISTIQ
. Moreover, the site includes battery production, stamping operations and dedicated wildlife conservation areas, reflecting GM’s broader sustainability strategy.
Texas: Home of GM’s Full-Size SUVs
Located in
Arlington, Texas
,
Arlington Assembly
specializes in full-size SUVs.
Production includes the
Cadillac Escalade
,
Escalade-V
,
Chevrolet Tahoe
,
Chevrolet Suburban
,
GMC Yukon
, and
GMC Yukon XL
. Since opening in 1954, the facility has produced more than
13 million vehicles
, making it one of GM’s most important manufacturing plants.
GM’s Manufacturing Network Extends Beyond Assembly Plants
While
GM assembly plants
perform final vehicle assembly, they represent only one part of the company’s manufacturing ecosystem.
Stamping facilities, powertrain plants, battery factories and component suppliers all work together to support production across the United States. Overall, this integrated manufacturing network enables General Motors to efficiently build a diverse range of gasoline, hybrid and electric vehicles while maintaining high production volumes.
