RACING
Isack Hadjar Calls Sepang Race a Disaster After P5
Published
18 minutes ago
on
October 5, 2026
By
Marcelo Lagos
Isack Hadjar lost a potential podium late in the 2026 Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia after grip problems and a battery issue left his Red Bull fighting to defend fifth place.
Hadjar saw a podium disappear at Sepang
Isack Hadjar left
Sepang deeply frustrated
after losing a podium finish in the closing stages of the Bahrain Grand Prix in Malaysia.
The Red Bull driver started eighth after a five-place grid penalty. He then moved through the field and spent much of the race running in fourth, putting himself in position for a possible podium.
Hadjar ultimately finished
fifth
, scoring 10 points. His teammate Max Verstappen won the race, giving Red Bull its first Grand Prix victory of the 2026 season.
Hadjar struggled with grip and balance during the final stages at Sepang.
The biggest problem was pace.
Hadjar said his Red Bull suddenly became much slower than it had been during the rest of the weekend. He described the car as
“horrible to drive”
and said something clearly was not working as expected.
The numbers support his frustration.
During the 30 green-flag laps before the late-race sequence, Hadjar lost an average of
0.881 seconds per lap to Verstappen
, according to the information supplied for the race.
Hadjar did not believe the power unit itself was the main problem.
Instead, he pointed toward the
chassis and lack of grip
as the primary cause of his loss of performance.
A battery problem cost Hadjar the podium
The situation became even worse after the final Safety Car restart.
Hadjar inherited third place when George Russell retired with a power-unit problem. That left him with a short opportunity to secure his first podium of the season.
But the Red Bull driver suddenly found himself without enough battery energy.
Hadjar said the issue appeared during the first two laps after the restart. He could not deploy enough energy on the straights and became vulnerable to the Ferraris.
Lewis Hamilton passed him first.
Charles Leclerc followed soon afterward.
Hadjar therefore dropped from third to fifth in just a few laps. The official results confirm his final position, only
8.571 seconds behind Verstappen
.
Hadjar finished fifth after starting eighth and briefly moving into a podium position.
The young Red Bull driver was especially disappointed because the weekend had started so strongly.
Hadjar qualified
third
, which was his joint-best qualifying result of the season. His grid penalty then moved him back to eighth for the race.
He made the most of the opening conditions.
Hadjar chose intermediate tires while several cars ahead started on slicks. He then moved forward before overtaking Liam Lawson on Lap 6 to take fourth.
From there, his pace was no longer enough to attack the leaders.
That made the late battery problem even more costly. Hadjar believed he could have kept third without the loss of energy after the restart.
Red Bull still closes the gap to McLaren
There was at least one positive for the team.
Verstappen’s victory delivered
25 points
, while Hadjar added another 10. Red Bull therefore scored 35 points at Sepang.
The result moved Red Bull closer to McLaren in the Constructors’ Championship.
After the race, Red Bull has
298 points
, compared with 316 for McLaren. The gap is now only
18 points
.
Hadjar’s individual position also improved.
He now has
86 points
in eighth place in the Drivers’ Championship. His 2026 season includes three podium finishes, with the latest coming in Azerbaijan before his fifth-place result at Sepang.
Hadjar wants a response in Singapore
Hadjar’s conclusion was blunt.
Despite Red Bull taking its first victory of the year, he described his own race as
“a disaster”
and said the team needs to understand what went wrong.
The next opportunity arrives quickly.
The championship now moves to
Singapore
, where Hadjar will be looking to recover after a frustrating weekend at Sepang.
The
Isack Hadjar Sepang disappointment
was ultimately caused by two problems at the worst possible moment: a lack of grip from the chassis and a late battery issue that left him unable to defend on the straights.
