NOW TRENDING
Mazda CX-6e Has an External Speaker That Can Swear
Published
28 minutes ago
on
October 5, 2026
By
Marcelo Lagos
The Mazda CX-6e adds an unusual external speaker system that can warn pedestrians, communicate with traffic and even play custom messages outside the vehicle.
The CX-6e has a very unusual feature
The
Mazda CX-6e
is an electric SUV with a feature rarely seen in production vehicles: an external speaker that lets the driver communicate with people outside the car.
The system is designed as an
External Announcement
function. Drivers can play predefined messages through a speaker mounted at the front of the vehicle.
That can have practical uses.
For example, the system can tell a distracted pedestrian to look up. It can also allow one driver to tell another to proceed through a confusing situation.
The Mazda CX-6e is an all-electric midsize SUV developed with Changan and sold in selected international markets.
The problem is what happens when drivers move beyond those polite messages.
Recent reports from Australia showed that the system can also be used for
rude or offensive announcements
. The vehicle allows drivers to select stored phrases and create their own messages through its large touchscreen.
That creates an unusual problem for an otherwise sensible safety feature.
A car speaker can become a digital megaphone
The CX-6e’s external audio system was designed to improve communication around the vehicle.
Drivers can use it to warn pedestrians or make their intentions clearer in traffic. The idea is particularly interesting for electric vehicles, which already rely heavily on exterior sound for pedestrian awareness.
But the same hardware can be used in very different ways.
Tests in Australia found that the system can deliver
custom voice recordings and profane messages
. That turns a safety-oriented feature into something closer to a digital megaphone.
The front end features Mazda’s illuminated signature wing and the hardware associated with the CX-6e’s advanced technology.
The technology itself is not the only interesting part of the CX-6e.
Mazda developed the SUV with
Changan Automobile
as part of its global electric-vehicle strategy. The CX-6e uses a 78-kWh lithium-iron-phosphate battery and a rear-mounted electric motor producing
190 kW
, or about 255 horsepower.
The Australian version offers up to
484 km of WLTP range
, while DC fast charging can replenish the battery from 30 to 80 percent in about 15 minutes under stated conditions.
The CX-6e is packed with technology
The external speaker is only one part of the SUV’s technology package.
Inside, the CX-6e has a
26.45-inch 5K touchscreen
, voice and gesture controls and a 23-speaker audio system rated at 1,280 watts.
The cabin also uses a minimalist design influenced by the Japanese concept of
“ma,”
which Mazda describes as an approach centered on the beauty of empty space.
The CX-6e combines a minimalist cabin with a large digital display and advanced connectivity features.
The SUV also offers a large head-up display and a range of driver-assistance technologies.
Mazda says the CX-6e uses a combination of cameras, radar and ultrasonic sensors for systems such as autonomous emergency braking, lane support and rear cross-traffic alerts.
The result is a vehicle that relies heavily on software.
That is also why the external announcement system stands out. A feature controlled through software can offer useful communication, but it can also create unexpected behavior when users find alternative ways to use it.
Will Mazda restrict the speaker system?
Mazda Australia has not indicated that it plans to remove the feature.
However, the controversy surrounding the CX-6e’s external announcements highlights a broader issue with connected vehicles. Features created for convenience can have unintended uses once drivers gain access to customizable software.
The situation could eventually lead to
software restrictions or new limits on the types of messages that can be played outside the vehicle
.
For now, Mazda’s official Australian documentation presents the system as a communication and convenience feature. The inappropriate uses emerged from how drivers interacted with the available functionality.
The CX-6e is not a U.S.-market Mazda
There is another important point for American buyers.
The
Mazda CX-6e is not currently offered by Mazda USA
. Mazda has officially announced the model for Europe, Australia and other international markets, while Mazda’s current U.S. lineup does not include it.
That means the external speaker story is currently more relevant to international markets than the United States.
Still, the feature provides an interesting glimpse at where vehicle software is heading.
Modern EVs can communicate with their surroundings in increasingly sophisticated ways. The CX-6e shows that this can be useful, but it also creates new opportunities for drivers to misuse technology.
The
Mazda CX-6e external speaker
is therefore one of the strangest features appearing on a modern EV: a system designed to improve communication that can also become a very loud way to settle an argument in traffic.
