Verdict
GM’s 2027 Bolt delivers real value—255 miles, 150 kW charging, and $28,995 pricing—but its 18-month lifeline exposes EV vulnerability to tariffs and policy. Ideal for budget-conscious commuters needing a proven hatchback before stocks vanish mid-2027; avoid if longevity matters. GM prioritizes ICE profitability, leaving the affordable EV future uncertain—watch for Ultium scaling elsewhere.
Frequently Asked Questions
What is the base price of the 2027 Chevrolet Bolt EV?
The base price for the 1LT trim is $28,995, with the LT with Comfort Package at $29,990, RS Base at $32,995, and RS with Tech Package & Panoramic Roof at $35,685.
What is the EPA range and charging capability of the 2027 Bolt EV?
It offers 255 miles of EPA range and 150 kW DC fast charging, powered by a 65 kWh LFP underbody-mounted battery with 157 kW motor power.
When does production of the 2027 Chevrolet Bolt EV start and end?
Production started in November 2025 at the Fairfax Assembly plant in Kansas and will end around mid-2027, lasting approximately 18 months to prioritize ICE models like the Equinox and Buick Envision.
Are 2027 Chevrolet Bolt EVs available now?
Yes, production has started, first units are heading to dealerships now, and deliveries began early 2026 as 2027 model-year vehicles.
How does the 2027 Bolt EV compare to the original in terms of improvements?
It features upgrades like improved 150 kW charging speed from Equinox EV tech, cost-saving LFP batteries enabling sub-$30,000 pricing, and 255 miles EPA range, compared to the first-gen’s similar range but with battery recall issues.
