Use Michigan’s official county finder
Open the Michigan Counties directory
The state page lists all 83 counties and links to county government websites.
Select the county where the land is located
Do not select a county from the property owner’s mailing address.
Find Equalization, GIS or Property Search
County terminology differs, but these pages frequently provide parcel maps, assessment links or local-unit contacts.
Identify the city or township
The assessment record and current tax bill may be controlled by that local unit rather than the county.
Use only portals linked from government pages
Local units frequently use hosted property systems. Confirm that the official government website sent you to the portal.
Open all 83 Michigan counties
Open Michigan property-tax resources
Choose the property task you need to complete
Each section routes the property to the office, search or form that controls the next action.
Michigan office-routing guide
Which office controls the property record?
Correct Michigan office for each property task
Office or board
Main responsibility
Contact it for
Important limit
City or Township Assessor
Prepares and certifies the local assessment roll.
Parcel card, assessed value, taxable value, classification, property characteristics, PRE and local corrections.
Usually does not collect delinquent taxes or provide legal title opinions.
County Equalization Department
Reviews local assessment levels, studies sales and supports countywide equalization.
Countywide parcel or GIS access, sales studies, local assessor contacts, equalization ratios and assessment support.
It may not replace the local assessor as the assessor of record.
Local Treasurer
Collects current summer and winter taxes for the local unit.
Current bill, due date, payment, receipt, deferment and local late charges.
Collection dates and payment vendors vary among municipalities.
County Treasurer
Collects taxes after they are returned delinquent and administers statutory delinquency procedures.
Prior-year balance, interest, forfeiture, redemption, foreclosure and payment-plan questions.
It does not ordinarily change the property’s assessed value.
Register of Deeds
Records and preserves deeds, mortgages, liens, releases, easements and plats.
Grantor-grantee index, document image, certified copy, recording date and legal-description research.
An online document search is not a complete title examination.
Local Board of Review
Reviews assessment protests and specified exemption or correction requests.
March valuation protest, poverty exemption, qualified errors and other matters allowed by law.
July and December meetings are not a general second opportunity to appeal value.
State Tax Commission
Oversees assessment administration and statewide equalization.
State forms, classification appeals, assessor standards, taxable-value reports and equalization guidance.
It is not the ordinary local parcel-search or tax-payment office.
Michigan Tax Tribunal
Hears formal property-tax and exemption appeals.
Timely valuation, exemption, classification, special-assessment and other qualifying appeals.
Strict filing deadlines apply and petitions are not accepted by ordinary email or fax.
Fast routing rule:
Parcel facts or value = local assessor. Countywide map or sales study = Equalization. Current tax = local treasurer. Delinquent tax = county treasurer. Deed or lien = Register of Deeds. Formal appeal = Board of Review followed by the proper state appeal route.
Parcel, address and owner lookup
Search Michigan property records correctly
Michigan parcel formats and online search systems differ by county and municipality. Start with the parcel number when available, use fewer search terms and always verify the local assessing unit.
Parcel-number search
Copy the number from a tax bill, assessment notice, deed or prior property card.
Enter the complete number first.
Remove dashes, spaces or dots when the portal rejects the format.
Try the base or parent number after a recent split or combination.
Confirm the municipality before relying on the result.
Best exact identifier when available
Address search
Use the property’s physical address.
Start with the street number and main street name.
Remove direction, suffix, unit and ZIP code if no result appears.
Search the street name alone for uncertain numbering.
Use GIS for vacant land without a standard address.
Simplify the address before adding fields
Owner-name search
Check whether the local system offers an owner field.
Start with the surname or legal business name.
Remove punctuation, middle initials and unnecessary words.
Try the trust, estate, LLC or previous owner.
Use the Register of Deeds for legal transfer confirmation.
Public owner access varies locally
Complete search workflow
Identify the county from the physical location
County boundaries do not always follow mailing cities, ZIP codes or school districts.
Identify the city or township
The same county can contain dozens of separate assessment and tax-collection units.
Open the official county or municipality website
Look for Assessor, Equalization, GIS, Parcel Search, Property Search or Tax Search.
Use one search field first
Do not combine an uncertain owner name, partial address and incorrect parcel number.
Open every plausible result
One owner can hold multiple houses, lots, farms, condominiums or commercial parcels.
Verify three identifiers
Match the parcel number, local unit and physical location.
Check the data-current date
A recent deed, land division or new construction item may be newer than the public property card.
Save the complete record
Keep the parcel number, assessed value, SEV, taxable value, PRE percentage, classification and tax information.
Information to save from the property card
County and local assessing unit
Parcel identification number
Property address
Owner name as displayed
Property classification
True cash or estimated market value
Assessed value and SEV
Capped and taxable values
PRE percentage
Land and building details
Transfer date
Summer and winter tax links
Hosted portal warning:
Many Michigan communities use an outside company to host parcel and tax data. Use the link provided by the official county, city or township website so you know the correct municipality and database were selected.
