Micro steps after selecting a county
Open the county’s official website
Look for Equalization, GIS, Parcel Viewer or Property Search.
Search or identify the local unit
Record whether the parcel is in a city, township or village.
Find the assessor of record
Use the local-unit directory when the county does not directly maintain the assessment search.
Find the correct treasurer
Use the local treasurer for current taxes and county treasurer for delinquent prior-year taxes.
Use the Register of Deeds for legal documents
Select the same county where the land is physically located.
Open the official county directory
Open the Register of Deeds directory
Failed-search recovery
No Michigan property record found?
Wrong local unit
Confirm whether the property is in a city, township or village rather than relying on the postal city.
Parcel format failed
Remove dashes, spaces and leading punctuation, then try the parent or prior parcel number.
Address failed
Remove direction, road suffix, unit number and ZIP code. Search only the number and main street word.
Owner search failed
Try surname only, business name, trust, estate, former owner or a Recorder search.
Recent purchase is missing
Search the seller and check the recorded deed. Assessment ownership updates may follow recording.
Parcel was split or combined
Search the original parent parcel, adjoining parcel numbers, legal description or land-division record.
Condominium record is missing
Search the unit number, condominium name, master parcel or legal unit description.
Vacant land has no address
Use GIS, owner name, parcel number, subdivision, plat or a neighboring parcel.
Manufactured home is missing
Determine whether it is assessed as real property, personal property or handled through another registration record.
Online access is limited
Some units do not expose every field publicly. Request the property record card from the assessor.
Prepare this before contacting the assessor
Complete physical property address
Possible parcel number
City, township or village
Current and former owner names
Approximate purchase date
Deed or instrument reference
Subdivision or condominium name
Nearby parcel or intersection
Assessor call script
“I am trying to identify the property at [physical location]. I have [possible parcel number, owner, deed reference or subdivision]. Can you confirm the assessing unit and whether the parcel was transferred, divided, combined, renumbered or recorded under another address?”
Michigan value decoder
Understand TCV, assessed value, SEV and taxable value
Common Michigan property-record fields explained
Field
What it means
What to check
Parcel ID
Local identifier assigned to the assessment parcel.
Use the same number for tax, map, deed and appeal research.
Local assessing unit
City or township responsible for the assessment roll.
Do not assume the county directly assesses the property.
True Cash Value
Michigan’s market-value concept based on the usual selling price.
Compare arm’s-length sales, property facts and condition.
Assessed Value
Generally 50% of true cash value before state equalization.
Review the annual assessment notice and property record card.
State Equalized Value
Assessed value after county and state equalization adjustments.
SEV is not necessarily the value used to calculate the tax bill.
Capped Value
Prior taxable value adjusted for the permitted inflation multiplier, additions and losses.
Verify additions, losses and transfer-of-ownership status.
Taxable Value
Value used to calculate ad valorem property taxes.
It may be below SEV until an ownership transfer uncaps it.
PRE percentage
Portion qualifying for Principal Residence Exemption treatment.
Confirm ownership, occupancy and the correct percentage.
Property classification
Agricultural, commercial, developmental, industrial, residential, timber-cutover or personal property classification.
Classification affects certain exemptions and appeal routes.
Millage
Tax rate expressed in dollars per $1,000 of taxable value.
Summer and winter bills can carry different levies.
Special assessment
Charge for a project or service that may be separate from ordinary ad valorem tax.
Contact the levying municipality or district for the calculation and purpose.
Transfer date
Ownership event that may affect uncapping in the following calendar year.
Confirm the deed and whether a statutory exception applies.
2026 capped-value formula
2026 Capped Value = (2025 Taxable Value − Losses) × 1.027 + Additions
The 2026 inflation multiplier is 1.027, or 2.7%. The taxable value can still be affected by additions, losses, transfer-of-ownership uncapping, corrections and the property’s SEV.
Do not compare taxable value directly with a sale price.
A long-held property can have a taxable value well below its SEV. A qualifying ownership transfer can cause taxable value to uncap in the calendar year after the transfer.
Check these facts before disputing value
Land size and classification
Building square footage
Year built and effective age
Condition and quality
Basement and finished area
Garage and outbuildings
Additions and losses
Transfer and uncapping status
PRE percentage
Qualified agricultural status
Open the official 2026 multiplier bulletin
Open the Michigan tax estimator
Current August 2026 action board
Important Michigan property dates still ahead
Rare equalization appeal
August 17, 2026
Limited multiplier-based appeal circumstances.
Summer tax due
September 14, 2026
Unless a city charter uses another date.
Late interest begins
September 15, 2026
Generally 1% per month, subject to local rules.
Winter PRE deadline
November 1, 2026
File Form 2368 with the local assessor.
Winter taxes
December 1, 2026
Generally become due and payable locally.
December Board of Review
Around December 15
Local unit may use another authorized date that week.
Commercial MTT deadline
June 1, 2026
The ordinary deadline has passed.
Residential MTT deadline
July 31, 2026
The ordinary deadline has passed.
Local dates can differ.
Charter cities can use different summer-tax dates, and each city or township publishes its own Board of Review schedule, meeting method and appointment procedure.
What to do right now
2026 summer bill is unpaid
Confirm the local due date and pay the local treasurer before the applicable interest schedule begins.
You may qualify for deferment
Contact the local treasurer immediately and request Form 471. Do not wait until the final statutory date.
Principal residence has no PRE
File Form 2368 by November 1 for winter-levy treatment when eligible.
Ordinary value appeal was missed
Ask the local assessor whether the issue is a qualified error, exemption, classification, arithmetic error or another matter with a separate deadline.
Tax bill has an arithmetic error
A limited Michigan Tax Tribunal route may require filing within 60 days after the bill was mailed.
Assessment notice or PRE denial just arrived
Use the notice-specific deadline rather than assuming the ordinary July 31 date applies.
Open the official 2026 calendar
Check summer-tax deferment
Tax lookup, calculation and payment
Find the correct summer, winter or delinquent tax bill
Current summer tax
Start with the city, township or other local collecting treasurer shown on the bill.
Current winter tax
Use the local treasurer and verify the local due date, payment methods and late-charge schedule.
Prior-year delinquent tax
Contact the county treasurer or designated foreclosing governmental unit for a current payoff.
Safe tax-search and payment steps
Find the correct parcel record
Save the parcel number, local unit and property address.
Determine whether the bill is current or delinquent
Current bills generally remain with the local treasurer; returned delinquent taxes move to the county.
Open the official local or county treasurer page
Use a payment link provided by the government website rather than a search-engine advertisement.
Select the correct tax year
A public portal may display several summer, winter and prior-year records.
Check PRE and taxable value
A missing or incorrect PRE can materially affect school operating tax.
Separate ad valorem tax from special assessments
Drain, road, lighting, water, sewer and other charges may require a different office.
Review payment fees
E-check, card, telephone and counter-payment charges vary by local unit and vendor.
Save the confirmation
Keep the parcel number, tax year, season, amount, date and confirmation number.
Verify posting
Reopen the public account after processing and keep the receipt until the balance updates.
Estimate the tax before paying
Estimated Ad Valorem Tax = Taxable Value × Total Millage ÷ 1,000
One mill equals $1 of tax for every $1,000 of taxable value. The final bill can differ because of PRE treatment, administration fees, special assessments, credits, delinquency and different summer or winter levies.
Example:
A taxable value of $100,000 multiplied by 36 mills produces $3,600 in estimated ad valorem tax before special assessments, fees or other adjustments.
Use the state estimator
Open Michigan’s Property Tax Estimator
Select the tax year supported by the tool.
Select the county
Choose the county where the property is physically located.
Select the local unit and school district
Millage depends on the exact taxing jurisdictions.
Enter taxable value—not market value
Copy taxable value from the current property record.
Select PRE status accurately
The estimator can be materially wrong when PRE treatment is entered incorrectly.
Treat the result as an estimate
Use the live treasurer bill for the actual amount payable.
Missing bill warning:
Not receiving a bill does not ordinarily eliminate the tax or applicable late charges. Search the official account and contact the treasurer before the deadline.
Treasurer call script
