Purchases, transfers and uncapping
Why a Michigan buyer’s taxes may rise
A qualifying transfer of ownership generally causes taxable value to uncap in the calendar year after the transfer. The new taxable value can become equal to the property’s state equalized value, subject to applicable law and exemptions.
Buyer warning:
Do not estimate future taxes from the seller’s current bill alone. The seller may have a long-capped taxable value that does not continue after the transfer.
Buyer tax-estimate workflow
Find the current SEV
Use the official local property record and confirm the assessment year.
Check the current taxable value
A large difference between taxable value and SEV signals possible capped-value savings.
Identify the transfer type
Sale, trust, family, entity and estate transfers do not all receive the same uncapping treatment.
Read the state transfer guidelines
Do not assume that a family or trust transfer is automatically exempt from uncapping.
Estimate using the likely post-transfer taxable value
Use the state estimator with the correct local unit, school district and PRE status.
Budget for summer and winter taxes
The total annual levy may be divided between two bills.
Check for special assessments
These charges can continue independently of ownership and assessed value.
