Ten practical answers
Michigan property record and tax FAQs
Do Michigan county auditors provide property searches?
Michigan generally does not use a county auditor as the primary parcel-search office. Start with the city or township assessor or the county Equalization Department. Use the local treasurer for current taxes, county treasurer for delinquent taxes and Register of Deeds for recorded documents.
Is there one statewide Michigan property-search portal?
No single state portal contains every public parcel record. Michigan property records are maintained locally. Identify the county and city or township, then use the official local property, assessment or GIS search.
Can I search Michigan property by owner name?
Many local property systems offer owner-name searches, but availability and formatting differ. Start with the surname, remove punctuation and verify the parcel address. Use the Register of Deeds when legal ownership or a recent transfer must be confirmed.
What is the difference between assessed value, SEV and taxable value?
Assessed value is generally 50% of true cash value. State Equalized Value is the assessed value after equalization. Taxable value is the value used to calculate property taxes and may be lower than SEV until a transfer of ownership causes uncapping.
When are Michigan summer property taxes due in 2026?
The statewide calendar lists September 14, 2026 as the general summer-tax due date, although a charter city may use another date. Interest generally begins September 15 unless a different local charter rule applies.
What is the 2026 Michigan taxable-value multiplier?
The 2026 inflation rate multiplier is 1.027. The general capped-value formula is the 2025 taxable value minus losses, multiplied by 1.027, plus additions.
What is the Michigan Principal Residence Exemption?
The Principal Residence Exemption can exempt an eligible owner’s principal residence from up to 18 mills of local school operating tax. It is separate from the Michigan Homestead Property Tax Credit.
Where can I search Michigan deeds and liens?
Use the Register of Deeds in the county where the land is located. Search grantor and grantee names, document type, recording date, instrument number, liber and page when those fields are available.
Can I still appeal my 2026 Michigan property value?
The ordinary 2026 Michigan Tax Tribunal deadlines for most commercial property and residential or agricultural property have passed. July and December Boards of Review may correct only specified errors or consider certain exemptions. Contact the local assessor immediately to identify any remaining route.
Who collects delinquent Michigan property taxes?
Current-year taxes are generally collected by a city, township or other local treasurer. After taxes are returned delinquent, the county treasurer or another designated foreclosing governmental unit handles payment, forfeiture and foreclosure procedures.
